{"parentUuid":"c51f5d48-847b-459b-ba14-bd54a7e24aae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HMuSKQXwPaZMuq168ThQD5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"nauty Traces bliss saucy graph automorphism tool comparison 2024 2025 benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Nauty Traces – Home\",\"url\":\"https://pallini.di.uniroma1.it/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmark Graphs for Practical Graph Isomorphism\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1705.03686\"},{\"title\":\"nauty and Traces: Graph Isomorphism Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://hunterheidenreich.com/notes/interdisciplinary/graph-theory/nauty-traces-graph-isomorphism/\"},{\"title\":\"Saucy3: Fast Symmetry Discovery in Graphs\",\"url\":\"http://vlsicad.eecs.umich.edu/BK/SAUCY/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmark Graphs for Practical Graph Isomorphism - DROPS\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/opus/volltexte/2017/7870/pdf/LIPIcs-ESA-2017-60.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Search Space Contraction in Canonical Labeling of Graphs Adolfo Piperno\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0804.4881\"},{\"title\":\"saucy (+) and nauty (×) slowdowns on dense graphs. The programs were... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/saucy-and-nauty-slowdowns-on-dense-graphs-The-programs-were-executed-on-the_fig2_2896494\"},{\"title\":\"nauty and Traces User’s Guide (Version 2.6) Brendan D. McKay∗\",\"url\":\"https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/nauty/nug26.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Practical graph isomorphism, II | Journal of Symbolic Computation\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1016/j.jsc.2013.09.003\"},{\"title\":\"Practical graph isomorphism, II Brendan D. McKay\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1301.1493\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding graph automorphism tools comparison:\n\n## Overview of Tools\n\nnauty and Traces are programs for computing automorphism groups of graphs and digraphs, and they're compared alongside other tools like Bliss, Saucy, and Conauto.\n\n## Recent Version Information\n\nVersion 2_9_3 of nauty and Traces has been released on JAN 1, 2026, making it the most current version available.\n\n## Benchmark Comparisons\n\nnauty is generally fastest for small graphs and some easier families, while Traces dominates on most difficult graph classes, sometimes by orders of magnitude.\n\nMore specifically:\n\n- Traces' breadth-first tree scanning strategy and richer node invariant provide large gains on graphs with complex symmetry structure, but conauto and Bliss have specialized handling for graph families with many disjoint or minimally-overlapping components.\n\n- Saucy is extremely efficient on some families of graphs which mainly come from encodings of the satisfiability problem, but usually slower than other tools outside those classes, and saucy does not compute a canonical labeling.\n\n## Algorithmic Approaches\n\nnauty dominated the field for decades until competitors like saucy (2004), Bliss (2007), and conauto (2009) introduced sparse data structures, early refinement abort, and other improvements.\n\nThe search results don't contain specific 2024-2025 benchmark comparisons, but they reference comprehensive comparison frameworks and indicate that no single tool dominates all graph classes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"01982267-7272-46ba-8d39-0890712da45a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:29.722Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c51f5d48-847b-459b-ba14-bd54a7e24aae","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7837f327-cad9-4265-92b7-c5aa555d979b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HhNWiqbiBGps7MStfjzNqB","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SCIP symmetry handler 2024 2025 bliss orbital fixing lex-leader\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SCIP Doxygen Documentation: How to use symmetry handling in SCIP\",\"url\":\"https://www.scipopt.org/scip/doc/html/SYMMETRY.php\"},{\"title\":\"The SCIP Optimization Suite 9.0 Suresh Bolusani · Mathieu Besan¸con\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/scipopt-90-2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The SCIP Optimization Suite 9.0\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.17702v2\"},{\"title\":\"A Computational Comparison of Symmetry Handling ...\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/5209.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SCIP Optimization Suite 10.0: Exact Solving, Better Decompositions, and a More Productive Ecosystem | One trivial observation at a time\",\"url\":\"https://www.pokutta.com/blog/research/2025/12/02/scip-10.html\"},{\"title\":\"SCIP Doxygen Documentation: prop_symmetry.c File Reference\",\"url\":\"https://www.scipopt.org/doc/html/prop__symmetry_8c.php\"},{\"title\":\"The SCIP Optimization Suite 10.0\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.18580v1\"},{\"title\":\"The SCIP Optimization Suite 10.0 – Optimization Online\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/2025/11/the-scip-optimization-suite-10-0/\"},{\"title\":\"SCIP Doxygen Documentation: symmetry_orbital.c Source File\",\"url\":\"https://www.scipopt.org/doc-9.1.0/html/symmetry__orbital_8c_source.php\"},{\"title\":\"SCIP Doxygen Documentation: symmetry_orbital.c File Reference\",\"url\":\"https://www.scipopt.org/doc/html/symmetry__orbital_8c.php\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about SCIP's symmetry handler, bliss, and the related techniques you asked about:\n\n## SCIP Symmetry Handling (2024-2025)\n\nSCIP 9.0 substantially extends the ability to handle symmetries in three directions. First, more sophisticated techniques are available to handle symmetries of non-binary variables. Second, the mechanism to detect symmetries has been completely restructured.\n\n### Bliss and Automorphism Detection\n\nTo detect automorphisms of symmetry detection graphs, the previous version of SCIP made use of the graph automorphism software bliss, and SCIP 9.0 allows to make use of sassy, which preprocesses the symmetry detection graphs to accelerate the computation of symmetries, and can be used by compiling SCIP with option SYM={sbliss,snauty}.\n\nIn SCIP 10.0 (2025), there have been updates to the symmetry detection tools: The release of SCIP 10.0 adds another interface to the software dejavu. In contrast to previous versions, bliss is no longer shipped with SCIP; instead, nauty is provided with the SCIP release.\n\n### Orbital Fixing and Lexicographic Reduction\n\nOrbital reduction is a generalization of orbital fixing that also works for non-binary variable domains. Additionally, in SCIP, the same method is used for orbital reduction and for lexicographic reduction, which means that these two methods are compatible and can be used simultaneously in the same factor.\n\nLexicographic reduction is the dynamic counterpart of symresack and orbisack propagation.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a0909b87-c338-482b-8079-556a273cded5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:30.794Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7837f327-cad9-4265-92b7-c5aa555d979b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"65bd639b-b90e-4b3c-8e95-11e15c076e34","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GRFq4Ns1jfjqH5vMZMNrFw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT or-tools symmetry detection automatic perron 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"custom search constraints and symmetry breaking with cp/sat\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/4tqNoPhFnAw\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"A Novel Approach For Detecting Symmetries in CSP Models | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-68155-7_14\"},{\"title\":\"ViolationLS: Constraint-Based Local Search in CP-SAT | Integration of Constraint Programming, Artificial Intelligence, and Operations Research\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-3-031-60597-0_16\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"SymmetryPropagator | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/reference/sat/symmetry/SymmetryPropagator\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"symmetry breaking when building a model with python · google/or-tools · Discussion #3295\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/discussions/3295\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about CP-SAT, OR-Tools, symmetry detection, and Laurent Perron (a key researcher in this area). Here's what I found:\n\n## CP-SAT and Symmetry Detection\n\nThe `SymmetryPropagator` class detects potential literal propagations based on problem symmetries in OR-Tools. This class implements more or less the strategy described in the paper: Devriendt J., Bogaerts B., De Cat B., Denecker M., Mears C. \"Symmetry propagation: Improved Dynamic Symmetry Breaking in SAT\", 2012, IEEE 24th International Conference on Tools with Artificial Intelligence.\n\n## Automatic Symmetry Detection in CP-SAT\n\nThe search results show that CP-SAT automatically performs symmetry detection during preprocessing, generating symmetry graphs with nodes and arcs, computing generators and orbits, and identifying orbitopes.\n\n## Laurent Perron's Role\n\nPerron provided a follow-up of a CPAIOR 2020 masterclass presentation on CP-SAT and OR-Tools. Perron is a key figure in the development and research of constraint programming solvers at Google.\n\nThe search results also mention that symmetry breaking work in CP-SAT was ongoing, with plans to address this area eventually.\n\nHowever, I did not find specific 2025 publications about \"CP-SAT or-tools symmetry detection automatic perron\" in these results. The most recent references to 2025 in the results relate to other developments in the OR-Tools ecosystem.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"dc642e94-dd47-44a9-9408-40b0f81b632b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:31.873Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"65bd639b-b90e-4b3c-8e95-11e15c076e34","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7149bd3f-e6e3-48d2-bdb6-5631bbdbca4e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014m5WedAdUjTAfSCZ7WqiL8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT CSP automatic symmetry detection colored graph saucy bliss 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Algorithms Transcending the SAT-Symmetry Interface\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2306.00613\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Graph Symmetry Detection and Canonical Labeling: Differences and Synergies\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/230764359_Graph_Symmetry_Detection_and_Canonical_Labeling_Differences_and_Synergies\"},{\"title\":\"Runtimes of saucy 3.0, saucy 2.1, and bliss 0.72 as a function of graph... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Runtimes-of-saucy-30-saucy-21-and-bliss-072-as-a-function-of-graph-size_fig6_230764357\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Symmetry Detection and Canonical Labeling: Diﬀerences and Synergies\",\"url\":\"https://easychair.org/publications/open/2vbW\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - markusa4/sassy: the sassy preprocessor for symmetry detection · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/markusa4/sassy\"},{\"title\":\"Improved static symmetry breaking for SAT\",\"url\":\"https://www.bartbogaerts.eu/articles/2016/003/ImprovedStaticSymmetryBreakingSAT.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Symmetry Propagation: Improved Dynamic Symmetry Breaking in SAT\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/236587867_Symmetry_Propagation_Improved_Dynamic_Symmetry_Breaking_in_SAT\"},{\"title\":\"Exploiting Structure in Symmetry Detection for CNF\",\"url\":\"http://vlsicad.eecs.umich.edu/BK/SAUCY/conf/saucy-dac04.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Engineering a Preprocessor for Symmetry Detection - DROPS\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.SEA.2023.1\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Encoding of Partial Ordering Models for Graph Coloring Problems\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol305-sat2024/LIPIcs.SAT.2024.12/LIPIcs.SAT.2024.12.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about SAT/CSP symmetry detection and the tools you mentioned:\n\n## Symmetry Detection in SAT/CSP\n\nIn practice, symmetries are detected by modeling a given SAT formula as a graph, and then applying an off-the-shelf symmetry detection tool, such as saucy, to the resulting graph.\n\n## Saucy and Bliss Tools\n\nThe search results contain comparisons between these two prominent symmetry detection tools:\n\nSaucy outperforms bliss in the search for automorphisms. More specifically, of the 432 total benchmarks, saucy solved 431 in less than 5 seconds and processed one instance of SAT 2011 CNFs in 102 seconds, while bliss timed out on 27, and solved the remaining in 727 seconds.\n\n## Recent Developments\n\nState-of-the-art solvers for symmetry detection include nauty, bliss, Traces, and dejavu, which were initially designed with inputs from combinatorics in mind, while saucy focuses on practical instances that exhibit sparsity. Additionally, a preprocessor for symmetry detection has been engineered, designed to shrink sparse, large substructures of the input graph, which improves the overall running time significantly for many of the state-of-the-art solvers.\n\nThe search results also include a 2024 SAT paper on graph coloring problems that discusses symmetry-breaking constraints, but don't provide specific 2024-2025 developments specifically focused on the combined themes of your query.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"dda09f19-ecc5-4b1a-be36-e13f1e7f2223","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:32.650Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7149bd3f-e6e3-48d2-bdb6-5631bbdbca4e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
