{"parentUuid":"31f0d515-7581-41ca-914e-339b4e5c36e0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012gJeTs2zW7NcCrQxaPvwPa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MNNXZdYF6QTQ3uoHUYFf7W","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"empty rectangle placement grid symmetry D4 dihedral group breaking lex constraint"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5692,"cache_read_input_tokens":21939,"output_tokens":362,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5692},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":362,"cache_read_input_tokens":21939,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5692,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5692,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caozawg6tArTugqDSKXnb","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"841fab2a-6597-464e-a938-864876006f2a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:39.278Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c3e4fc73-c2c3-4ec3-8727-3b74c3516705","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HohdfaDtG7hLi3iZ7zVmo2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"dejavu graph automorphism solver 2024 2025 sassy preprocessor\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Download · dejavu\",\"url\":\"https://automorphisms.org/download/download/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - markusa4/sassy: the sassy preprocessor for symmetry detection · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/markusa4/sassy\"},{\"title\":\"Documentation - dejavu\",\"url\":\"https://www.automorphisms.org/documentation/\"},{\"title\":\"DejaVu · Julia Packages\",\"url\":\"https://juliapackages.com/p/dejavu\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - markusa4/dejavu: fast probabilistic symmetry detection on graphs · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/markusa4/dejavu\"},{\"title\":\"dejavu::solver Class Reference - dejavu\",\"url\":\"https://www.automorphisms.org/documentation/classdejavu_1_1solver.html\"},{\"title\":\"Quick Start - C++ API · dejavu\",\"url\":\"https://automorphisms.org/quick_start/cpp_api/\"},{\"title\":\"dejavu\",\"url\":\"https://automorphisms.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarks · dejavu\",\"url\":\"https://automorphisms.org/graphs_benchmarks/benchmarks/\"},{\"title\":\"Engineering a Preprocessor for Symmetry Detection\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.06351\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about DejaVu, the graph automorphism solver, and the sassy preprocessor:\n\n## DejaVu Overview\n\nDejaVu is a Monte Carlo solver and library for the fast detection of symmetry, freely available under the MIT license. The goal of dejavu is to compute the symmetries of a given graph.\n\n## SASSY Preprocessor\n\nThe sassy preprocessor is designed to shrink large, sparse graphs. Before giving a graph to an off-the-shelf symmetry detection solver (such as bliss, dejavu, nauty, saucy, Traces), the graph is instead first handed to the preprocessor.\n\n## Recent Research\n\nThe underlying algorithms are based on a series of papers by Markus Anders and Pascal Schweitzer, including \"Engineering a Preprocessor for Symmetry Detection, SEA 2023, additionally co-authored by Julian Stieß.\"\n\n## Performance\n\nTaking the sum of all the runtimes of a graph class, dejavu is fastest on 32 classes, with Traces being fastest on 8, saucy on 3, and nauty on 1 (out of 44 total classes).\n\nThe search results reference versions through 2.0preview1 and include benchmarks comparing DejaVu to other state-of-the-art automorphism solvers, though specific 2024-2025 updates were not found in these results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0b3a8442-936c-4d19-9ffc-57bb5653c009","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:43.301Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c3e4fc73-c2c3-4ec3-8727-3b74c3516705","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0e43b011-8341-4121-af13-b9357bffee80","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019bGkDPxnLqzynzomEQMfvZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT or-tools detect_symmetry parameter symmetry_level orbitope\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: Parameter for symmetry finding dtime limit · Issue #4419 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4419\"},{\"title\":\"Google OR-Tools: Google.OrTools.Sat.SymmetryProto Class Reference\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/dotnet/classGoogle_1_1OrTools_1_1Sat_1_1SymmetryProto.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"SymmetryPropagator | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/reference/sat/symmetry/SymmetryPropagator\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT, UNKNOWN status with complete solution hint · Issue #3750 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3750\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Log - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/understanding_the_log.html\"},{\"title\":\"custom search constraints and symmetry breaking with cp/sat\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/4tqNoPhFnAw\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/symmetry.h at main · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/main/ortools/sat/symmetry.h\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the CP-SAT solver's symmetry detection and parameters:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n### Symmetry Level Parameter\n\nSymmetry finding can be forced on for large problems by setting symmetry_level:3. However, it will time out after a hard-coded 1 second of deterministic time, which can limit its effectiveness on larger problems.\n\n### Orbitope Detection\n\nAn orbitope is a special symmetry structure of the solution space. The search results show that CP-SAT detects and utilizes orbitopes as part of its symmetry detection process. For example, log output shows messages like \"Found orbitope of size 6 x 2\", indicating that the solver identifies orbitope structures during preprocessing.\n\n### detect_symmetry Parameter\n\nWhile the search results reference symmetry detection in CP-SAT, they confirm that symmetry detection is a built-in feature that involves detecting permutations and generating symmetry-breaking constraints. The `SymmetryPropagator` class detects potential literal propagations based on problem symmetries. When a literal is assigned, it checks if a symmetrical literal can also be propagated.\n\nThe results indicate that there are discussions about exposing parameters in sat_parameters.proto to allow configuring options like deterministic time limits for symmetry finding.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a01be5f6-5ad0-4ff2-8315-127a90893836","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:44.732Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e43b011-8341-4121-af13-b9357bffee80","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"31f0d515-7581-41ca-914e-339b4e5c36e0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MvMe3emWC7nPTSQDU8rENj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MIP integer program colored graph symmetry detection construction encoding Margot\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"QUBO Formulations for MIP Symmetry Detection\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15070\"},{\"title\":\"A Computational Comparison of Symmetry Handling ...\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/5209.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry detection in Mixed-Integer Conic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://docs.mosek.com/whitepapers/symmetry.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"arXiv:0710.3603v3 [cs.DM] 7 Jul 2009 Ann Oper Res manuscript No.\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0710.3603\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Symmetry in Integer Linear Programming | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Symmetry-in-Integer-Linear-Programming-Margot/9df2db1c2b2f788acbbad152630a91fdb5d76693\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry detection for MIPs in MOSEK 10\",\"url\":\"https://themosekblog.blogspot.com/2022/05/symmetry-detection-for-mips-in-mosek-10.html\"},{\"title\":\"New Integer Linear Programming Models for the Vertex Coloring Problem∗\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1706.10191\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry Detection for Quadratic Optimization Using Binary Layered Graphs\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9717/7/11/838\"},{\"title\":\"Engineering a Preprocessor for Symmetry Detection\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.06351\"},{\"title\":\"The Graph Coloring Problem: Exact and Heuristic Solutions | Towards Data Science\",\"url\":\"https://towardsdatascience.com/the-graph-coloring-problem-exact-and-heuristic-solutions-169dce4d88ab/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings related to MIP symmetry detection, colored graphs, and the reference to Margot:\n\n## MIP Symmetry Detection and Colored Graph Encoding\n\nFormulation symmetry detection can be converted to a graph theoretic problem by representing the MIP formulation as a bipartite vertex- and edge-colored graph, where vertices in one partition represent MIP variables with colors corresponding to their objective coefficients, vertices in the other partition represent MIP constraints with colors representing their constant terms, and edges are colored based on variable coefficients from the constraints. The automorphism group of this graph is equivalent to the formulation symmetry group, so finding MIP symmetries reduces to finding graph automorphisms.\n\n## Quantum Computing Approach\n\nQUBO (Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization) models are being explored as the canonical format for quantum optimization platforms, with researchers developing full, reduced, and decomposed QUBO as well as QUBO-Plus formulations for MIP symmetry detection.\n\n## The Margot Instances\n\nThe Margot instances are special combinatorial instances with large and very complicated symmetries, where any solver that does not handle symmetries will not be successful and symmetry handling requires more advanced techniques. On the Margot instances with their relatively complicated symmetry groups, isomorphism pruning, orbital fixing, and orbital branching are the fastest.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"097b651e-09f6-40a5-b347-7cdfbd07b756","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:45.260Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"31f0d515-7581-41ca-914e-339b4e5c36e0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"841fab2a-6597-464e-a938-864876006f2a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a7741c394a61d5aa4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MNNXZdYF6QTQ3uoHUYFf7W","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"empty rectangle placement grid symmetry D4 dihedral group breaking lex constraint\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Representation Theory of the Square: D4, Subgroups, and Decomposition - Carsten Jaeschke\",\"url\":\"https://carstenjaeschke.com/essays/representation-theory.html\"},{\"title\":\"Dihedral group of order 8 - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dihedral_group_of_order_8\"},{\"title\":\"3.3: Dihedral Groups (Group of Symmetries) - Mathematics LibreTexts\",\"url\":\"https://math.libretexts.org/Courses/Mount_Royal_University/Abstract_Algebra_I/Chapter_3:_Permutation_Groups/3.3:_Dihedral_Groups_(Group_of_Symmetries)\"},{\"title\":\"Breaking Symmetries from a Set-Covering Perspective | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-95973-8_11\"},{\"title\":\"Dihedral Group D_4 -- from Wolfram MathWorld\",\"url\":\"https://mathworld.wolfram.com/DihedralGroupD4.html\"},{\"title\":\"Dihedral Group D4 and Its Symmetries | PDF | Group (Mathematics) | Group Theory\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/357702230/Cayley-Table-of-D4\"},{\"title\":\"Definition:Dihedral Group D4 - ProofWiki\",\"url\":\"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition:Dihedral_Group_D4\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 6 Symmetry Topics : 1. Symmetry and Groups\",\"url\":\"https://ccremsing.weebly.com/uploads/1/2/0/8/120802094/ccr-ln_gg__-_ch._6.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MAS3219/MAS8219 Groups, Graphs and Symmetry Semester 2, 2012/2013\",\"url\":\"https://www.mas.ncl.ac.uk/~najd2/teaching/mas3219/notes.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Category:Dihedral Group D4 - ProofWiki\",\"url\":\"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Category:Dihedral_Group_D4\"}]\n\nI've performed a web search for your query on \"empty rectangle placement grid symmetry D4 dihedral group breaking lex constraint.\" The results provide substantial information about the D4 dihedral group and symmetry breaking, though no results specifically discuss empty rectangles or the lexicographic constraint in that exact context.\n\nHere's what I found:\n\n## D4 Dihedral Group\n\nD4 is the dihedral group of degree 4 and order 8. It is the symmetry group of a square. Its symmetries form the dihedral group D4, which includes 4 rotations (around 0, 90, 180, 270 degrees) and 4 reflections (2 over the axis halving diagonals and 2 over the diagonals going through opposing corners).\n\n## Symmetry Breaking\n\nThe search results include research on symmetry breaking in constraint programming. For breaking symmetries on graphs, a permutation covers a graph if applying it yields a smaller graph in a given order. Canonical graphs are those that cannot be made smaller by any permutation. A complete symmetry break is a set of permutations that covers all non-canonical graphs. Additionally, optimal LexLeader symmetry breaks for graphs have been computed for graphs of order n≤10.\n\nThe results do not contain specific information about empty rectangles on a placement grid or the specific interaction between D4 symmetry and lexicographic constraints in that context. If you're working on a specific problem combining these concepts, you might need more specialized literature on combinatorial optimization or constraint solving.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7623cb61-e3cb-432e-a8b2-8d8f6fec3620","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:46.118Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"841fab2a-6597-464e-a938-864876006f2a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
